# against the install set it claims to install (docs/design/packaging.md §6.3).
# See debian/README.source before building.

Source: xtgeoip
Section: net
Priority: optional
Maintainer: xtgeoip packaging <packaging@example.invalid>
# ^ Replace before uploading anywhere. Whoever uploads a package owns this
#   field, and it is not upstream. `.invalid` is reserved by RFC 2606, so the
#   address above cannot be delivered to by accident.
#
# No C compiler is listed although §3 of the design note says every recipe
# needs one: `aws-lc-sys` is compiled for the TLS backend. On Debian gcc comes
# from build-essential, which Policy §4.2 makes implicit for every source
# package, and Policy §7.6 says not to list it. cmake is deliberately absent —
# aws-lc-sys takes its pregenerated-source `cc` path on the architectures
# Debian builds for, measured 2026-09-19.
Build-Depends:
 debhelper-compat (= 13),
 cargo,
 rustc (>= 1.85),
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://github.com/HomerSlated/xtgeoip
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/HomerSlated/xtgeoip.git
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/HomerSlated/xtgeoip
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: xtgeoip
Architecture: any
Depends:
 ${shlibs:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
 xtables-addons-common,
Recommends: xtables-addons-dkms | xtables-addons-source
Suggests: logrotate
Description: GeoLite2 database builder for the xt_geoip iptables match
 xtgeoip downloads MaxMind's GeoLite2 country CSV database and converts it into
 the binary per-country IP range files that the xt_geoip netfilter match reads,
 letting iptables and nftables rules select traffic by country. It is a Rust
 reimplementation of xt_geoip_build_maxmind(1) from xtables-addons-common.
 .
 It detects the remote version before downloading, so a run that finds nothing
 new transfers nothing; it archives and prunes previous datasets; and it stores
 the MaxMind account credentials encrypted rather than in plain text.
 .
 The data files it produces do no matching by themselves. That is done by
 xt_geoip: the userspace extension in xtables-addons-common, which this package
 depends on, and the kernel module, which on Debian comes from
 xtables-addons-dkms.
 .
 There is no packaged /etc/xtgeoip.conf. That file holds the encrypted
 credentials and is rewritten in place, so it belongs to the operator rather
 than to the package manager. Run "xtgeoip conf --show" first: it offers to
 create the configuration from the example this package installs under
 /usr/share/xt_geoip/.
